e next chapter the  index that kind of thing now we don't  really use the web like that anymore  but the link header is still generically  useful for linking together resources  that are semantically related and the  most popular use of this header today is  preload which is to say this other  resource is going to be needed in order  to render this page so please load it as  soon as possible you do it like this  it's commonly used for fonts because  fonts can be discovered very late in the  process of loading a document you need  to scan the page discover the style  sheets load the stylesheet then discover  the font declaration  so you still possibly don't load the  font at that point because you need to  know whether the font is being used  somewhere on the page so fonts can  actually be kicked off very late in the  waterfall if you want them to be loaded  a lot earlier then link preload is a  good way of doing that this is a this is  from a case study by audios Marnie at  Google that shows how adding link  preload affects the tinder website and  you can see that  we start with some of their JavaScript  loading at around the one-second mark  and I'm talking there about when we  start to load the JavaScript not when  it's finished after we add the preload  tags all the JavaScript starts to load  at the same time around the 200  millisecond mark so we can substantially  increase the speed at which we get to a  useable interactive site by using  preload the problem is that we have to  wait until the server sends a status  code before we can send headers so  preload substantially improves the  performance we can start to load our  fonts a lot earlier but we still can't  load them as early as we'd like to and  here's an illustration of why so when I  type in a web address and press Enter  the first thing the browser does is it  makes DNS lookup for that hostname then  it negotiates a tcp + TLS connection  then it sends the request then it waits  for the server to think and the server  is going to be doing some database  access and authentication templating  maybe reaching out some other micro  services assembling a response and  ultimately the server will decide this  is a 404 all this is a 200 or whatever  the H the correct HTTP response code is  and then once it's decided that it can  then emit a header block and then it can  start streaming the response which it  might still be building so we already  support the idea of built of streaming  responses but we have to wait for that  status line before we start to do that  so firstly we're working on the new spec  which we call early hints this will be a  new HTTP status code and the description  of this status code is basically - no  yet so you would omit this status code  very quickly after receiving the request  before you've done any server-side  processing and it would give you the  opportunity to send some headers and  most notably it would give you the  opportunity to send link preload headers  then the browser can start loading those  critical resources before we've decided  whether this is going to be a 200 or not  so now the waterfall can look like this  we can start loading those  resources whilst the server is still  considering the the original request and  we haven't yet received the first bytes  of the response now also within  performance we have measurement related  headers any performance engineer will  tell you that sort of 50% of the  challenge of performance is measurement  and one of the the best additions to the  platform in this area has been the  server timing header so server timing  allows us to start exposing some of  what's going on during that server think  time which we are addressing with the  early hints status code we can start to  try to reduce the amount of server think  time by understanding where we are  spending that time are we spending in  the database are we spending it in  memcache are we spending that that in  you know HTTP requests to other servers  this is the syntax it's quite flexible  you can attach a millisecond timestamp  to a label or you can just omit a label  or you can omit a label on a description  in Chrome this data actually gets  rendered as part of the timing panel of  dev tools this the only browser  currently to visualize server timing and  it's pretty rudimentary one of the  limitations of server tiny is that  there's no order information so you can  see that although we have a waterfall in  the the main data here for server timing  data it's all just stacked up on the  right that's because there's no  information within server timing that  tells us that this happened before this  or this blocks that part of the reason  for this is that server timing is  expected to be generated across multiple  pieces of physical infrastructure with  different clocks so it would be  ineffective solution to include  timestamps  we could add some kind of mechanism for  saying this before that or this blocks  that or something like that and  hopefully that will be in a later  remission of this spec what's really  interesting to me about server timing is  that it has a JavaScript API so within  the page we can actually get access to  the data that we're sending and then you  can beacon that data back to some  collection server or aggregation server  and  actually this is one of the first  instances where we have a JavaScript API  into the data that we're receiving in  our HTTP response headers most of the  time it within the page when the jobs  could be run on the page we can't really  see the data that is in the HTTP header  section really the only other instance  is cookies but cookies have side-effects  they they you know they have a  client-side state so what if we were to  use this to just send arbitrary data to  the browser this is really useful for a  lot of reasons one reason is that many  large sites will generate relatively  static pages and then save them in an  edge cache or you know some kind of  cache that is is very hot and close to  the end user for the performance but  they want to enhance these pages with a  little bit of extra data which  personalizes them something like the  number of number of pages left on your  pay wolf on your pay wall credit for  example so imagine that you could add  headers to the response to add that  extra information and then you could use  JavaScript to reassemble it now I'm not  saying this is a good idea it's  definitely a misuse of the header but  it's interesting that this is this is  currently the only header would actually  let you do this I want to finish with  mentioning a couple of headers which  don't really exist and use it will form  yet feature policy is shipped in Chrome  in fact chrome is kind of racing ahead  of the rest of the web on this one of  the problems that we have with a web  that is now 30 years old is that it's  big the platform is huge and so the  attack surface is huge the range of API  is you can inadvertently trigger as huge  and some of the features that it exposes  are really powerful  so wouldn't it be great if we could  selectively turn off bits of the web  platform that we know we're not going to  use either because we just want to avoid  any potential vulnerabilities that might  exist in those API s or because we want  to self-police we know that we have a  large development team  and we don't want anyone to to use a  feature that we've decided we don't want  to use let's say for example you've  decided in a matter of policy that your  website will not also play video on page  load that's something you'd have to  police with code reviews and so on or  you could write a feature policy that  just says autoplay not so now the  ability to autoplay video does not exist  on the web  within this page this is really powerful  I love this feature and it's a framework  like client hints so we can add more and  more policies as the best practice or  what we consider to be best practice on  the web evolves as of today there are 10  reasonably well agreed policies which  are listed on left here and you'll see  these if you go to mdn and search for  feature policy but like I said chrome is  kind of racing ahead and if you actually  go into Chrome Canary enabled  experimental web features and query for  the allowed features they actually  shipped quite a lot of additional  features which have not yet really been  discussed in great detail in the  standards community but some of these  are great ideas like document right for  example though so if you set document  right none then document right doesn't  exist as an API on your page that's for  you and also for any third parties you  log into the page and you know I recall  in a previous job we had all sorts of  trouble trying to get advertisers to  stop using document or write as a  mechanism to add their content to the  page this would be a very simple way of  solving that problem instantly so I'm  very excited about this I'm less excited  about the fact that this is going to  turn into a bit of a content security  policy problem this is currently the  tightest feature policy you could set  and as more and more policies are added  this would just get longer so hopefully  at some point there will be some  consolidation and you'll be able to  reference a policy like you know best  practice web 2019 or something and that  will aggregate quite a lot of these  policies together the Google amp team  have specifically highlighted feature  policy as one of the things that they  need in order to move amp from  being a proprietary solution to one that  standards-based and the final thing is  origin policy and I'm bit sad about  origin policy it was it's been proposed  for over a year and just doesn't seem to  be getting very much interest but the  idea of origin policy is to take you  know this this situation where we had  this really clean response and this  became this really long and messy  response to take this and get back to  that simple response again because if  you think about all the headers that  we've talked about today very few of  them actually relate to the specific  response we're about to receive so  something like strict Transport Security  for example is manifestly an origin  level setting because it doesn't matter  which response I attach it to it will  apply from then on to my entire origin  some things are theoretically response  specific like content security policy or  referrer policy but in practice no one  really has a different content security  policy for every page they have one for  their whole site so it makes sense to  take a lot of this stuff and move it out  of individual HTTP responses and into  something which maybe we might call an  origin policy and it could be a JSON  file that we store at a well-known  location and just reference in every  HTTP response so we can separate the  stuff which is common across all  responses from the stuff which is  specific to this response now the  reality is this doesn't actually improve  performance very much if at all because  with HTTP two we now have header  compression and the header compression  dictionary is connection level so when  we make a second response if we include  the same headers that we included on the  first response then the size will be  basically zero because the connect the  the connection already has a compression  dictionary that contains that content so  it's not going to improve performance  very much but it does improve developer  experience because you're able to  segregate the headers which are origin  specific from those which are response  specific so I've covered a lot of  headers today this is kind of a little  cheat sheet for  to take home and think about the the top  left ones are ones that you know apart  from future policy are ones which if you  don't have for them already you probably  should there are very few use cases that  don't call for CSP or strict Transport  Security or refer a policy or link  preload at the same time in the bottom  right if you're still serving any of  these headers maybe think about whether  you whether you really still need those  if you want more detail on any of this  or indeed more headers because I really  have only covered a very few of them  then this blog post is a good place to  start this is the ones that I would like  to see less of and there's a link at the  bottom of this to a sec as a part two  where we cover the the ones the good  ones and you can fetch you can get the  slides from my SlideShare account and  that's it thank you  do we have I guess if you have questions  feel free to come and talk to me
 

